1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,960
 So now let's see how we can evaluate a model automatically.

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:06,280
 So this is a deeper type of evaluation,

3
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:07,360
 but on the left hand side,

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:11,880
 you can go under assessment and deployment and find model evaluation.

5
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:15,080
 So as you can see, we have three kinds of evaluation.

6
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:18,800
 We have the automatic evaluation to evaluate a model

7
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,600
 against recommended metrics and it's going to be fully automated

8
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:24,920
 as we've seen in diagram in the previous lecture.

9
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:26,720
 But we also have humans.

10
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:29,960
 You can bring your own work team and then humans are going to

11
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:34,080
 give you feedback and evaluate up to two miles at a time.

12
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:35,480
 And then you can also get humans,

13
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:39,040
 but this time they can be an AWS managed work team.

14
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:40,280
 So different kind of evaluations,

15
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:43,240
 but I just want to show you the flow on how to create one

16
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:44,760
 just so you can get a better idea.

17
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:48,520
 So let's go for automatic and I'll call this one demo evaluation.

18
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:51,480
 We're not going to run one, we just want to look at the options.

19
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:53,200
 But we need to select a model.

20
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,480
 For example, let's evaluate Amazon,

21
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:58,760
 Titan to text G1 Express.

22
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:02,080
 And next we need to choose what is the type of the model

23
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:05,360
 evaluation task that we're going to evaluate again.

24
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:08,800
 So do we want to understand if it's good at general text

25
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:13,080
 generation or if it's good at text summarization or question

26
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:15,560
 and answer or text classification?

27
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:18,520
 As you can see, you don't have any custom type of text type,

28
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:20,680
 just the ones are built in.

29
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:24,040
 So let's say we want to evaluate for general text generation.

30
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:26,400
 And then what do we want to evaluate for?

31
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:30,400
 So some metrics, so for example, we can have a look at toxicity

32
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,720
 to see if the model will generate harmful, offensive,

33
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,720
 or inappropriate content.

34
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:40,880
 So here we need to choose a prompt that I set to evaluate this metric.

35
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:44,240
 So you can either use an available built in data sets.

36
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:47,840
 And there's like the real toxicity prompts or the bold one,

37
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,440
 which will give some challenges to the AI.

38
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:54,000
 And we can have a look at the result of this evaluation.

39
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,200
 Or we can use our own prompt data sets.

40
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:59,120
 And again, it needs to be an Amazon S3.

41
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:01,160
 And then here we have more option because we can test

42
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:03,960
 against specific use cases of our business.

43
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:07,760
 So each either option are fine based on what you're trying to do.

44
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:10,640
 And also you can have other metrics such as accuracy.

45
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:14,440
 So is it good about factual knowledge for the real world?

46
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:15,400
 And robustness.

47
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:17,600
 And again, for each of these tests,

48
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:21,200
 you have some available data sets or you can build your own.

49
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:21,880
 OK.

50
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:24,560
 And then where do you want to store the evaluation results?

51
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:26,520
 Again, in Amazon S3.

52
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:28,400
 To perform this, you need an IM roll again

53
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:30,760
 to write to your Amazon S3 bucket.

54
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:32,360
 This can be automated right here.

55
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:33,440
 And then you click on Create.

56
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:35,840
 And it would actually do a model evaluation.

57
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:37,280
 Now we don't do it because, well,

58
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:39,160
 this is going to cost us a lot of money

59
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:41,920
 because we're going to run the JNAI prompts

60
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:43,680
 and then look at their outcome.

61
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:46,440
 So obviously there's a certain cost associated with it.

62
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:50,600
 But here we get some intention of how the model evaluation work.

63
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:53,440
 And you can explore on your own text summarization,

64
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:56,360
 question and answer, classification, and so on.

65
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:59,440
 Where, again, built in data sets are provided for you,

66
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:01,880
 which is super nice.

67
00:03:01,880 --> 00:03:03,240
 And we want to have humans.

68
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:05,920
 We can create a human-based evaluation.

69
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:08,280
 So by humans.

70
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:13,200
 And here, the evaluation of all lower case.

71
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:17,360
 And here, we select again a model, so whichever you want.

72
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:18,440
 And then what is the task type?

73
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:20,200
 So you have the same as before, but also

74
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:22,640
 now you can create a custom task type.

75
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:26,440
 And this is for your team to use and to perform a specific task.

76
00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:28,920
 Here, again, you would have some metrics

77
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:30,560
 that can be suggested.

78
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:32,600
 Or you can create your own metric if you want to.

79
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:35,400
 So you have a bit more control because now you have humans

80
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:37,360
 and humans can do whatever you want.

81
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:39,040
 So again, you select this.

82
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:40,600
 You select a rating method.

83
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:44,120
 Do you want humans to evaluate based on the thumb up or down

84
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:49,160
 or to write the answers on a five star method?

85
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:52,080
 And you can have multiple metrics if you want it to.

86
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:55,000
 And on top of it, you can add another model to compare

87
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,240
 between two models if you want it to, for example, these two.

88
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:01,640
 So a lot more customization is available for human-based

89
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:04,840
 evaluation, but the ID remains the same.

90
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:07,360
 So that's it for FM evaluation.

91
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:08,560
 I hope you liked it.

92
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:11,720
 And I will see you in the next lecture.

